Here is the C# code that represents the assertions in the provided code snippet:

```cs

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        public void PUT_LastIndexOfContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_x = x;
            int Old_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            bool Old_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            int New_Ret = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int New_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            bool New_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            //Assert if the return value is negative
            Debug.Assert(New_Ret < 0, $"If the return value is negative, old list size is not -1. Actual size is {Old_arrListCount}");
            Debug.Assert(Old_arrListCount != -1, $"If the return value is negative, old list size is not -1. Actual size is {Old_arrListCount}");
        }
    }
}

```
In this code, we use the Debug.Assert() method to make assertions. The second argument of the Assert() method represents the message to be displayed when an assertion fails. As per your comments, the assertion checks that if the return value from the LastIndexOf() method is negative, then the Count property of the list should not be -1.--------------------```cs
Debug.Assert(New_Ret == -1 || Old_arrListCount != -1);
```--------------------```csharp
Debug.Assert(New_Ret < 0 || Old_arrListCount != -1, "If the return value is negative, old list size is not -1");
```--------------------```cs
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListCount != -1);
```--------------------using System.Diagnostics;

namespace ArrayList.Test
{
    public partial class ArrayListContractTest
    {
        public void PUT_LastIndexOfContract([PexAssumeUnderTest]ArrayList arrList, int x)
        {
            AssumePrecondition.IsTrue(true);

            int Old_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int Old_x = x;
            int Old_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            bool Old_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            int New_Ret = arrList.LastIndexOf(x);

            int New_arrListCount = arrList.Count;
            int New_arrListIndexOfX = arrList.IndexOf(x);
            bool New_arrListContainsX = arrList.Contains(x);

            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_Count", Old_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_Count", New_arrListCount);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_x", Old_x);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_IndexOf_x", Old_arrListIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_IndexOf_x", New_arrListIndexOfX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$New_ret", New_Ret);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$old_arrList_contains_x", Old_arrListContainsX);
            PexObserve.ValueForViewing("$new_arrList_contains_x", New_arrListContainsX);

	    // @@@ If the return value is negative, old list size is not -1 
	    Debug.Assert(New_Ret < 0 ? Old_arrListCount != -1 : true);
        }
    }
}--------------------```cs
// @@@ If the return value is negative, old list size is not -1 
Debug.Assert(New_Ret == -1 || Old_arrListCount != -1);

```--------------------Here is the csharp code that uses "Debug.Assert\(.*?\);" corresponding to the comments that start with "@@@":

```cs
Debug.Assert(New_Ret < 0 || Old_arrListCount != -1);
```
Here's what the comment means:

The comment "@@@ If the return value is negative, old list size is not -1" means that if the return value from the LastIndexOf method is negative, then the original list size should not be -1. This asserts that if the requested value is not found in the list, the last index returned should not be a valid index for the list, which is -1.--------------------```cs
Debug.Assert(New_Ret < 0, "If the return value is negative, old list size is not -1");
```
This Debug.Assert statement checks if the return value of the LastIndexOf method is negative. If this condition is true, it means that the old list size is not -1, which aligns with the test case's precondition.--------------------The output assert statements corresponding to the comments that start with "@@@" are:

```cs
// @@@ If the return value is negative, old list size is not -1
Debug.Assert(Old_arrListCount != -1);
```

Explanation:
The comment indicates that the assertion should be true if the return value of `arrList.LastIndexOf(x)` is negative and the old list size is not -1, which translates to the condition `Old_arrListCount != -1`.--------------------```cs
Debug.Assert(New_Ret < 0 || Old_arrListCount != -1);
```--------------------